<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>This fictional modern episode addresses the presenting concerns below. [S]</paragraph><list><item>Her periods are so painful that she misses work, and she wants to know what is wrong. [S]</item><item>She wants children one day and is afraid that the cause of her pain, or an operation, will prevent it. [S]</item><item>She feels low and sleeps badly on painful nights, and does not want to depend on pills. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>She was born Norma Jeane Mortenson on June 1, 1926, at Los Angeles General Hospital. Her father&apos;s identity is unconfirmed; her mother was often confined to asylums, and she spent her childhood in orphanages and foster homes. [D]</item><item>She worked as a photographic model, signed with Twentieth Century-Fox in 1946 under the name Marilyn Monroe, starred in films of the 1950s including Gentlemen Prefer Blondes and The Seven Year Itch, and studied at the Actors Studio in the mid-1950s. [D]</item><item>Her mother, Gladys, was diagnosed with paranoid schizophrenia and was institutionalized, for the first time at the state hospital in Norwalk. [D]</item><item>She is described as one of the most famous people with endometriosis. Her biographer Anthony Summers wrote that the condition was so severe that it destroyed her marriages, her wish for children, her career and ultimately her life; a review of endometriosis for clinicians quotes this account, and museum and medical-history writers list endometriosis among her health problems. [D]</item><item>The same biographical account holds that, in the days before effective conservative surgery or medical therapy, her endometriosis led to progressively increasing use of strong analgesics, tranquilizers and hypnotics, and to drug dependency. [R]</item><item>The physician and historian Howard Markel writes that she had several mental health problems, including substance abuse, depression and, most likely, bipolar disorder, as well as endometriosis and gall bladder disease. [R]</item><item>Most of the drugs she misused had been prescribed to her by physicians; they included the barbiturate Nembutal and chloral hydrate. [D]</item><item>A retrospective psychological autopsy based on biographies, films and her writings proposes autism spectrum traits progressing to borderline personality disorder and bipolar disorder; the roster lists borderline personality disorder and depression. [R]</item></list><paragraph>3 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

